          %p
            %a#DocStart
          %h1#title Locally Kinematic Spaces
          %h5#author Andrew Stacey
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#introduction"} Introduction
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#mapping_spaces"} Mapping Spaces
              %li
                %a{:href => "#local_structure"} Local Structure
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related Concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          #sectiona.num_section
          %h2#introduction.num_section Introduction
          %p
            The various categories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalised+smooth+spaces"} generalised smooth spaces
            were introduced to correct defects with the category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds+with+corners"} manifolds with corners
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+dimensional+manifolds"} infinite dimensional manifolds
          %p However, having built a suitable nice category, the “nice objects” (that is to say, the manifolds) are still inside it and it is a natural question as to how to identify these nice objects in amongst the nasty ones. The obvious answer is tautological: the manifolds are those objects that I first thought of. The real problem with this is that it is an external characterisation. A better characterisation would be one that could take an object from one of these categories and examine it purely within that category and conclude whether or not it was a manifold. This might lead to more things being called a manifold than were originally thought of, but should give a characterisation better knit to what a manifold actually is than just what happens to be our current best description thereof.
          %p
            An example of this sort of result is
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Frölicher spaces
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Sta11%5D%28%23citeStacey3%29%2C+Proposition+8.3%5D+where+it+was+shown+that+in+the+category+of+%5B%5BFroelicher+space"} ?
            %em categorically
            pick out
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_1.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %p
            The purpose of this page is to study a possible description of manifolds. The description is motivated by the construction of
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                manifolds of smooth maps
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/manifolds+of+smooth+maps"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+addition"} local addition
            on the target space. Therefore our first approximation is that of
            %em locally additive
            spaces, namely spaces that are equipped with a local addition.
          %p
            The rough idea behind a local addition is simple. In any of the categories of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalised+smooth+spaces"} generalised smooth spaces
          %p The rest of the data in a local addition says that this modelling must vary smoothly over the space. This translates into the transition functions being smooth.
          %p Thus a locally additive space is locally smoothly modelled in its tangent spaces. The key difference between this and the notion of a smooth manifold is that a locally additive space is somewhat tortoise-like in that it carries its model spaces around with it, rather than having them imposed from outside.
          #sectionb.num_section
          %h2#definition.num_section Definition
          %p
            We assume throughout that we have fixed upon one of the categories of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalised+smooth+space"} generalised smooth space
          %p
            The page
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+addition"} local addition
            has the definition of a local addition for a smooth manifold. This is almost the same definition for us here, except that our underlying space is a generalised smooth space and we don’t assume that the source is a vector bundle. We shall also work with the most precise version. Putting all of this together, we shall use the following definition.
          #defna.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_2.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a smooth space. A
              %em local addition
              on
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_3.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a smooth map
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_4.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta \colon T X \to X
              with the following properties:  
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                #enumaZa.num_enuma
                %p
                  The composition of
                  %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_5.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi η
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
                  with the zero section is the identity on
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_6.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %li
                %p
                #enumaZb.num_enuma
                %p
                  There is an open neighbourhood, say
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_7.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi V
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                  %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_8.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times X
                  such that
                  %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_9.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi π
                        %mo ×
                        %mi η
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi T
                        %mi X
                        %mo →
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \times \eta \colon T X \to X \times X
                  induces a diffeomorphism with image
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_10.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi V
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %li
                %p
                #enumaZc.num_enuma
                %p
                  For
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_11.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                .maruku-equation
                  %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_12.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mi x
                        %mi X
                        %munder
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %mo ≅
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mn 0
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mi x
                        %mi X
                        %munderover
                          %mo →
                          %mrow
                            %mo ≅
                          %mrow
                            %mi d
                            %mi η
                        %msub
                          %mi T
                          %mi x
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        T_{x} X \xrightarrow[\cong ]{} T_{0} T_{x} X \xrightarrow[\cong ]{d\eta } T_{x} X
                %p is the identity.
          %p From this definition, that of a locally additive space is obvious.
          #defnb.num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em locally additive space
              is a pair
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_13.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \eta )
              where
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_14.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a smooth space and
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_15.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
              is a local addition on
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_16.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Although the obvious definition, as it stands it is not quite precise. This is because the various notions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+space"} tangent space
            diverge once one goes outside the realm of smooth manifolds. In this article, we shall use the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinematic+tangent+space"} kinematic tangent space
            %em locally kinematic space
            for a locally additive space where the tangent space used is the kinematic version.
          %p
            The other part of the definition that needs pinning down is the notion of an
            = succeed "." do
              %em open neighbourhood
          %ol
            %li
              %p
              #enumbZa.num_enumb
              %p The assignment of a topology to a smooth space is functorial, and
            %li
              %p
              #enumbZb.num_enumb
              %p
                The topology on
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_17.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
                is the standard one. These both seem quite reasonable! In fact, these two conditions define a particular topology which we refer to as the
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvaceous+topology"} curvaceous topology
          %p
            The really important property of the local addition is that it allows us to lift tangent vectors to curves. Of course, we can always do this for a single tangent vector (as we are using the kinematic tangent space) but a local addition allows us to do it for a family. As a direct consequence of this lifting, we get the following simple result. Recall that for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinematic+tangent+space"} kinematic tangent space
            at a point there are potentially two smooth structures: the quotient structure and the fibre structure. The lifting property of a locally additive space implies that these two are the same.
          #lemmaa.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_18.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\eta )
              be a locally kinematic space. For
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_19.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_20.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi x
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{x} X
              agree. That is,
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_21.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi x
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{x} X \to T X
              is an embedding.
          .proof
            %p
              Let us write
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_22.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi x
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{x} X
              for the tangent space at
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_23.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_24.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T X)_{x}
              for the fibre of
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_25.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X
              at
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_26.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %p
              We define a map
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_27.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X \to C^{\infty } (\mathbb{R},X)
              by sending
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_28.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in T X
              to the curve
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_29.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \mapsto \eta (t v)
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_30.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (\mathbb{R},X) \to T X
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_31.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T X)_{x}
              in to
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_32.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } ((\mathbb{R},0), (X,x))
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_33.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi x
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (T X)_{x} \to T_{x} X
              inverse to the given map
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_34.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi x
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{x} X \to (T X)_{x}
          #sectionc.num_section
          %h2#mapping_spaces.num_section Mapping Spaces
          %p
            The motivation for locally kinematic spaces comes from considering mapping spaces. All of the categories of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalised+smooth+space"} generalised smooth space
            are cartesian closed. This means that for
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_35.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_36.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            smooth spaces, the set of smooth maps
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_37.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,X)
            is again a smooth space. This construction is functorial and satisfies lots of nice properties.
          %p
            Let
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_38.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be a locally kinematic space, with local addition
            = succeed "," do
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_39.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_40.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subseteq X \times X
            be the corresponding neighbourhood of the diagonal, so that
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_41.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo ×
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \times \eta
            is a diffeomorphism on to
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_42.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_43.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,\eta )
            is a diffeomorphism from
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_44.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S, T X)
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_45.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S, V)
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_46.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S, V)
            is a subset of
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_47.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S, X \times X)
            which is diffeomorphic to
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_48.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,X) \times C^{\infty } (S,X)
            and
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_49.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,V)
            contains the diagonal. That
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_50.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo ×
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,\pi \times \eta ) = C^{\infty } (S,\pi ) \times C^{\infty } (S,\eta )
            takes the zero section of
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_51.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi T
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S, T X)
            to the diagonal is obvious. Hence
            %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_52.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,\eta )
            is almost a local addition on
            = succeed "." do
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_53.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,X)
          %ol
            %li
              %p
              #enumcZa.num_enumc
              %p
                A natural isomorphism
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_54.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo ,
                        %mi T
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi T
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,T X) \cong T C^{\infty } (S, X)
            %li
              %p
              #enumcZb.num_enumc
              %p
                That
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_55.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,V)
                is an open neighbourhood of the diagonal in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_56.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi S
                        %mo ,
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S, X \times X)
          %p
            As we are using the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinematic+tangent+space"} kinematic tangent space
            %em transformation
            relating to the first piece. The locally additive structure allows us to construct the inverse.
          #lemmab.num_lemma
            %h6#lemma_2 Lemma
            %p
              For
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_57.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a locally additive space, the natural transformation
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_58.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi TC
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    TC^{\infty } (S, X) \cong C^{\infty } (S, T X)
            %p is a natural isomorphism
          .proof
            %p
              We need to define an inverse for this map. This starts by defining a lift from
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_59.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S, T X)
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_60.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S, C^{\infty } (\mathbb{R},X))
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_61.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi T
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in C^{\infty } (S, T X)
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_62.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ×
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} \times S \to X
              by:
            .maruku-equation
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_63.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (t,s) \mapsto \eta (t \alpha (s))
            %p
              where
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_64.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \alpha (s)
              uses the
              = succeed "-action" do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_65.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_66.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T X
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_67.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,X)
              and thus, by quotienting, a tangent vector in
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_68.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,X)
              based at
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_69.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi α
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \mapsto \eta (0 \alpha (s)) = \alpha (s)
            %p
              To show that this is the required inverse, we need to evaluate the resulting tangent vector at some
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_70.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in S
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_71.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \mapsto \eta (t \alpha (s))
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_72.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_73.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mn 0
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi x
                      %mi X
                      %mo ≅
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi x
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{0} T_{x} X \cong T_{x} X
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_74.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi t
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \mapsto t \alpha (s)
              has derivative (at
              = succeed ")" do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_75.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_76.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha (s)
              again. Thus we have the image of this under
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_77.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 0
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi x
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi x
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \eta \colon T_{0} T_{x} X \to T_{x} X
              which is just
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_78.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha (s)
              again.
            %p Thus we have the required inverse.
          %p This result is the mathematics behind the statement above that a local addition allows us to lift families of tangent vectors to curves.
          %p The second missing piece is a little harder to pin down because without knowing precisely which category of smooth spaces we are working in, we will find it hard to know exactly how to define the topology on a smooth space, and thus to know how to work with it. Thus rather than a detailed study of when this occurs, we shall adopt the mathematician’s defence.
          #defnc.num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              A smooth space
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_79.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              is said to be
              %em smoothly compact
              if whenever
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_80.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq X
              is open then
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_81.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,U)
              is open in
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_82.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi S
                      %mo ,
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,X)
          %p With that in place, we can now fill in the missing pieces.
          #theorema.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_83.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a locally kinematic space and
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_84.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              a smoothly compact space. Then
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_85.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,X)
              is again a locally kinematic space.
            %p
              The tangent space of
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_86.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty } (S,X)
              at
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_87.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \colon S \to X
              consists of all maps
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_88.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi β
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta \colon S \to T X
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_89.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                      %mi β
                      %mo =
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi \beta = \alpha
          #sectiond.num_section
          %h2#local_structure.num_section Local Structure
          %p Having a local addition means that the smooth space is locally modelled on its tangent spaces. It turns out that this also puts some restrictions on what those tangent spaces look like.
          #theoremb.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_90.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a locally kinematic space. Then for
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_91.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_92.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi x
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{x} X
              is a vector space.
          .proof
            %p
              As shown at
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+addition"} local addition
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_93.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi x
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_{x} X
              of the vector space structure is the addition. We have the zero vector, scalar multiplication, and that the identities of a vector space are satisfied whenever everything is defined. So we need to define the sum,
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_94.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo +
                      %mi v
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u + v
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_95.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo ,
                      %mi v
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi T
                        %mi x
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u, v \in T_{x} X
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_96.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \to X
              where
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_97.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ⊆
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \subseteq \mathbb{R} ^{2}
              is a neighbourhood of the origin, such that
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_98.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial _{t}
              maps to
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_99.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              and
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_100.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial _{s}
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_101.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_102.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi s
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial _{s} + \partial _{t}
              will be the required sum.
            %p
              Let
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_103.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi T
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta \colon T X \to X
              be the local addition and let
              %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_104.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \subseteq X \times X
              be the corresponding neighbourhood of the diagonal. Consider the curve
              = succeed "." do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_105.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ↦
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \mapsto (\eta (t v), \eta (u))
              = succeed "," do
                %math#mathml_70627b1c91cd3978f289ce8c4c1d29a936031e46_106.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t = 0
